			}
		],
		"deviation": "ZEROTH, THE EXTENT IS A RANGE AND THE TABLE ABOVE IS ITS UPPER END. The plant's own bounding box at the defaults runs 0.47 m to 0.58 m across seeds, because the stems' lengths are a lognormal whose spread IS the subject; tools/rung-sheet.py builds at seed 7 and reports 0.47, tools/appearance.sh at seed 1 and gets 0.58. The CEILINGS are identical over that range -- a rung's ceiling is set by the pixels its near distance gives and that distance is floored at 0.30 m for every subject this small -- so only the error targets move, from 0.39/0.87/1.74/3.48 mm at the 0.47 end to 0.39/1.07/2.15/4.30 at the 0.58 end. EVERY TESSELLATION COUNT BELOW WAS CHECKED AGAINST THE TIGHTER END and holds there, so declaring the looser extent does not buy this ladder anything. THREE DEPARTURES, AND ALL THREE ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. FIRST, EVERY RUNG IS UNDER ITS CEILING AND THE SHARE IS NOW THE POINT: 13596, 6674, 1196 and 666 triangles against 43740, 10935, 2733 and 683 -- 31%, 61%, 44% and 98%. The version this revises spent 5% of rung 0's ceiling and the owner's finding was that a photoreal plant reading sparser than its own photograph with 95% of its budget unused is a finding rather than a pass. Every triangle of the difference is an ORGAN and not subdivision: the flowering stems now BRANCH, so the counts buy a cyme rather than a fan of bare stalks; there is a stem leaf at every fork and two more nodes below every fork, so no stem is bare for nine tenths of its length; there are nine basal leaves instead of five and eleven heads instead of seven; every stamen has an anther on it; and the head of carpels is drawn as the two dozen separate carpels it is. One tessellation count moved with them and for its own reason -- see the FIVE-SIDED SECTION below. THE MEASURED TRIANGLE SIZE FOLLOWED: rung 0 built to its error target now covers 50.2 px^2 a triangle at its near distance against the flower class's prior of 25 (range 12-64), where the version before it covered 311.6 and tools/ladder-audit.py --priors flagged it as SPARSER than the range by a factor of twelve. It is inside the range now and the flag is gone. The prior was not wrong about this subject; the subject was not being drawn. SECOND, THE FIVE-SIDED SECTION, which is a departure from what the previous version of this spec claimed rather than from the tool. It said `a three-faced prism on every round part (its own departure from a cylinder is 0.28 mm at the pedicel's radius)`, which is true of the PEDICEL, whose half-width is 0.05 of the corolla's radius -- 0.55 mm, giving 0.28 mm against a 0.39 mm target. It is NOT true of the PETIOLE, the thickest stalk on the plant at 0.09 of a blade's radius, 1.03 mm: three sides depart by 0.51 mm, which is 1.3 times rung 0's target. Five sides depart by 0.19 mm and hold it, so rung 0's stalks are five-sided and every coarser rung's are three. It is the one tessellation count this revision changed and it is a fix, not a spend. HOLDING FIVE SIDES THROUGH RUNG 2 WAS TRIED AND REJECTED, on the argument the segment count already makes for itself: a sub-pixel stalk changing section moves the OUTLINE MEASUREMENT more than it moves the geometry, so keeping the finer section could buy a tighter rung claim. It does, and barely -- the worst outline departure over three seeds and five parameter points goes from 0.0557 to 0.0466, which moves rung 0's swap from 21.0 m to 17.4 m and costs 640 triangles at each of two rungs. The segment count's version of the same trade was 19 mm of instrument noise against 1 mm of real movement and was worth taking; this one is not. Recorded rather than dropped, because a rejection nobody wrote down gets re-proposed. THIRD, THE RUNGS ARE STILL DRAWN FURTHER OUT THAN THEIR BANDS, and the cause is an instrument floor rather than a ladder choice. AgLod swaps a rung where its declared error subtends one pixel, so rung 1's band at 0.83 m requires an error of 1.07 mm -- 0.0018 of this subject's extent -- and the library measures a rung's error with AgMask across 256 pixels of the whole plant, 2.3 mm each: ONE PIXEL OF THE INSTRUMENT IS TWO ERROR TARGETS. Measured here, on a plant of buds and stalks alone whose only change between rungs is one icosphere subdivision and two prism faces -- about half a millimetre of real movement -- the outline departure reads 0.0184 of the extent at rung 1, at rung 2 and at rung 3, THE SAME NUMBER THREE TIMES. That is the floor and not the geometry. Filed as feedback/the-outline-departure-has-a-resolution-floor-and-a-thin-subject-is-all-floor-412ef5b9.md. AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS A DECISION THAT THIS REVISION REVERSES, on new numbers rather than a new opinion. The previous version declared NO coverage claim's outline (AgClaim.Claim.with_outline), because measured it came back LARGER than the shrink conversion it would replace at every rung. On this plant it does not, because the coverage each step GAINS grew -- a fuller plant fills in more when it is flattened -- while the outline it moves did not: 0.0557 against 0.0973 at rung 1, 0.0568 against 0.0673 at rung 2, and 0.0942 against 0.0835 at rung 3. All three are declared, at 0.058, 0.062 and 0.099. WITHOUT THEM THE LADDER DOES NOT WORK AT ALL, which is stronger than `they are tighter`: the three conversions put rung 1 at a LARGER error than rung 2 while it costs 5.6 times as much, so AgLod never selects it and AgConformance refuses the ladder for a dominated rung. The suite asserts both directions. RUNG 3'S DECLARED OUTLINE IS LOOSER THAN THE CONVERSION IT REPLACES and is declared anyway: the conversion is a MODEL of an isotropic shrink and the outline is a MEASUREMENT, and a generator does not get to keep the model because the model flatters it. The price is rung 3 held out at 42 m rather than 36 m, on a rung whose band ends at 6.6 m either way. RUNGS 1 AND 2 MEASURE ALL BUT THE SAME OUTLINE -- 0.0557 and 0.0568, a difference inside one raster pixel, and in the WRONG ORDER -- because both are at the floor, and their declared bounds are nevertheless ordered -- rung 2 removes strictly more than rung 1 does, so its error cannot be smaller, and that ordering is a fact about the geometry declared where the instrument cannot see it. LAST, THE STEP FROM RUNG 2 TO RUNG 3 IS ONLY 1.8x where the two above it are 2.0x and 5.6x. Rung 3 is mostly STALK: twenty of them -- eleven flowering axes and nine petioles -- at twenty triangles each are 60% of that rung, and three segments is the fewest that holds its 4.3 mm target. A plant with twenty stalks has twenty stalks at every distance; what it may not have is detail on them. AND AT THE TOP OF EVERY DECLARED COUNT the coarse rungs pass their ceilings: twelve flowers, twelve buds, twelve spent heads and twenty leaves build 36952, 16578, 3422 and 1958 -- 0.8x, 1.5x, 1.3x and 2.9x. The ceiling is derived from the DEFAULT plant's extent and such a plant is half as tall again, so its own ladder would be recomputed; recorded here because the ratio is the parameter range's and not the default's.",
		"tri_area_measured_px2": 50.2
	}
}
