  "source": "plate de7a83a1 (plant-03.jpg, plant/bramble, exp-20261005T000050Z-bramble-band-minus-one), the large sunlit terminal leaflet at plate box 0.60 0.53 0.80 0.66, its midrib taken from (2869, 3219) to (3420, 3392) plate px (577 px long). THE TAPER IS MEASURED from t = 0.3 to the tip: walking out from the midrib along its normal until the lit green lamina ends, both sides, at eleven stations; the two sides' half-widths averaged and normalised to the widest (t 0.3: 0.94, 0.4: 1.00, 0.5: 0.83, 0.6: 0.79, 0.7: 0.67, 0.8: 0.56, 0.9: 0.37, 1.0: 0.12), then scaled so the full width is 0.78 of the length, the commission's side-by-side width (`plant/bramble`'s LEAFLET_WIDTH) -- the plate's own ratio read 0.9 and is foreshortened by an unknown tilt, so the WIDTH is chosen and the TAPER measured. Below t = 0.3 the walk leaked into the overlapping lateral leaflet on one side and the leaf behind on the other, so the rounded base (0 to 0.25) is CHOSEN: an ovate leaflet narrowing to its petiolule. THE TOOTH COUNT IS MEASURED: the margin's half-width at 400 stations over t 0.30-0.95, less a 41-station running mean, has 26 tips on one side and 24 on the other, 40.0 and 36.9 a unit length; 38. THE DEPTHS ARE CHOSEN: the margin's oscillation read 0.042-0.048 of the length at its 90th percentile, which the lamina's own lighting inflates; 0.12 and 0.05 alternately (doubly serrate, the character the genus is keyed on, as `outline/birch-leaf-doubly-serrate` cuts it). The forward lean (-0.4, tips toward the apex) is chosen: a serrate tooth points forward.",
  "why": "a Rubus leaflet: broad-ovate, widest a little below the middle, drawn in to a short acuminate tip, doubly serrate with about 38 forward-leaning teeth a margin"
}
