		"deviation": "RUNG 0 IS 78 DRAW-COST UNITS AGAINST A CEILING OF 41 -- 1.9x, DECLARED -- and the ceiling, not the subject, is what is wrong. (The brief's reference, tools/rung-budget.py 0.01 --class flower, gives 24, and 78 is 3.25x of that: still inside 4x. The audit holds the built extent, 13 mm, so 41 is the number enforced.) The revision took rung 0 from 736 to 78 and plant/yellow-rattle's rung 0 from 22,116 to 2,376. WHERE 736 -> 78 CAME FROM: 368 of it was every face emitted twice back to back; the wall is now emitted once and declared two-sided. The rest is tessellation, measured, not argued: point-to-triangle distance from a 120 x 120 sampling of the analytic pouch (the generator's own profile and notch, the notch floor on the wall) to each candidate mesh, at the defaults. 12 rings x 16 segments: 0.15 mm, 368 triangles. 12 x 8: 0.45 mm, 184 -- eight segments alone cannot meet 0.39 mm because an inscribed octagon sags r (1 - cos 22.5 deg) = 0.47 mm inside the face's ellipse. Pushing every ring out by 2 / (1 + cos 22.5 deg) splits that sag either side: five even rings then depart 0.364 mm at 72 triangles; four rings 0.44-0.47 mm at 56; three 0.60 mm at 40. Eight segments is the floor, because four teeth need a vertex on every tip and every notch floor. So 72 is the cheapest wall that holds rung 0's error target with its teeth, and the mouth adds 6. WHY THE MOUTH COSTS SIX: a two-sided wall is baked per vertex, so the inside is given the outside's sky and nothing darkens it any more. The dark mouth -- the calyx's highest-contrast feature -- is carried as its own part: a six-triangle fan closing the pouch one ring below the teeth, its colour at the measured mouth-over-wall luminance 0.024. Without it the mouth would render as lit straw. CUT-OUT CARDS WERE CONSIDERED AND DO NOT FIT: AgOutline's LEAF forces the half-width to zero at the card's tip and serrates along the margins, so it cannot draw a mouth truncated at 0.6 of the pouch's width with four teeth across it; and a card has no inside, so the mouth -- a hole into a volume -- cannot be a cut-out at all (feedback/, this revision). WHY THE CEILING IS WRONG FOR THIS SUBJECT: the ceiling is the subject's pixel area over 16 px2, so it grows with the SQUARE of its size, while the fewest triangles that hold 1 px on a smooth closed body grow roughly LINEARLY (rings and segments each go as the square root of radius over pixel). They cross near 2.5 cm for this shape (rung-budget 0.025 gives 154 against about 150 needed). Below that, no smooth rounded subject meets both its error target and its ceiling, whatever its class -- and the class is not the lever: at 13 mm, flower gives 41, shrub 33, rock 30, made 27. RUNG 1 (62) sits outside the audit's budget but inside the rung sheet's band 0.6-1.2 m, whose ceiling is 10: 6.2x. It is kept at 62 because the commission holds its silhouette at 0.020 of the extent (measured 0.01330): the best three-ring lens, ring places and radii optimised over the suite's nine parameter points, reaches 0.0236, and every lens of 40 or fewer triangles (mixed ring counts included) 0.036 or worse. So a 0.020 silhouette costs about 22 triangles per calyx at the parent's rung 1; at 0.040 the lens would be 30-38. The coverage-outline guidance (measure the floor, declare only above it) was not exercised: this revision declares no outline."
	}
