  "axes": [
    "sorting",
    "grading"
  ],
  "why": "THE TWO AXES THIS TOOL CAN SAFELY SWEEP ON A HEAP, and the two it cannot is the more useful half of this entry. `sorting` decides whether thinning removes a large block or a small one, and `grading` decides where on the heap the large ones are, so between them they move what the coarse rungs lose. Both are dimensionless and neither changes the block COUNT, which is what keeps a sweep affordable. NOT `extent_m` AND NOT `clast_median_m`, and not because they do not matter -- they are the two that matter most. A heap's block count is `(extent / spacing)^2` and `spacing` is derived from the block diameter, so the count is quadratic in the RATIO of the two. This tool varies one declared range at a time with everything else at its default, which holds the other half of that ratio fixed: sweeping `clast_median_m` to its 0.03 m floor at the 3 m default extent asks for 3249 blocks, and sweeping `extent_m` to its 20 m ceiling at the default block size asks for 2200. Either is minutes per point with a transfer bake on the fine side. The sweep that IS wanted -- block size varied with the extent scaled to hold the population fixed -- is a COUPLED one, and a sweep file has no way to say it. `stone/boulder-clump`'s declared rung claims were measured that way by hand and the method is recorded in `notes/measuring-the-rockfall-off-rock-01.md`; this entry is what the tool can check them against unattended."
}
